WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:05.549
Even the most talented actors have found themselves
giving an awful performance.

00:00:05.549 --> 00:00:10.620
Can you name the acclaimed actor who was head-over-heels
for Adam Sandler dressed up as a woman?

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:16.970
Or the famous actor and talk-show host who
was legally forced to team up with a talking dinosaur?

00:00:16.970 --> 00:00:18.090
Keep watching for more!

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:23.900
For such an awful movie, "Battlefield Earth"
features some pretty amazing actors, including

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:26.600
Barry Pepper and John Travolta.

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:32.330
But if we had to pick the best actor to appear
in this piece of sci-fi garbage, it's hard

00:00:32.330 --> 00:00:34.050
to argue with Forest Whitaker.

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:40.180
The man won an Oscar for his powerful performance
as the insane dictator Idi Amin in "The Last

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:41.230
King of Scotland."

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:47.410
He was the essence of cool in "Ghost Dog:
Way of the Samurai," he tore at our heartstrings

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:54.210
in "The Crying Game," and he grabbed everybody's
attention with his leading role in "Bird."

00:00:54.210 --> 00:01:00.120
What's such a talented guy doing in one of
the worst-reviewed films of all time?

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:05.640
A movie based on a novel written by the man
who created Scientology, no less?

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:11.050
The answer is simple: he was delivering the
worst performance of his career.

00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:16.100
Playing Ker, an oversized alien with dreams
of getting rich, Whitaker is forced to drop

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:22.450
insults like "corporate crap heads" and earnestly
wonder why humans enjoy eating rats.

00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:28.480
If this were a comedy, maybe it would work,
but it's supposed to be deadly serious.

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:35.402
So when Whitaker delivers his lines in caveman
fashion, it comes off as unintentionally hilarious.

00:01:37.593 --> 00:01:39.990
“Stupid Humans”

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:45.100
His only saving grace is that he's partially
shielded by Travolta's insane performance

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:46.590
as the power-hungry Terl.

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:51.870
Of course, even if Whitaker was doing an amazing
job, it hardly would've mattered, as he's

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:55.010
buried under some pretty terrible makeup.

00:01:56.310 --> 00:02:00.570
Jesse Eisenberg wowed everyone when he played
Mark Zuckerberg in "The Social Network."

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:05.850
While we can argue all day about the accuracy
of David Fincher's film, there's no denying

00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:09.129
Eisenberg gave a powerhouse of a performance.

00:02:09.129 --> 00:02:11.810
He was even nominated for a Best Actor Oscar.

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:15.980
In addition to playing the social media mogul,
Eisenberg has left his mark on movies like

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:20.019
"The Double," "Roger Dodger," "The End of
the Tour," and "The Squid and the Whale."

00:02:20.019 --> 00:02:26.590
But every great actor has their cinematic
Kryptonite, and in Eisenberg's case, that's

00:02:26.590 --> 00:02:29.319
"Batman v Superman: Dawn of Justice."

00:02:29.319 --> 00:02:34.799
The curly-haired star won a Worst Supporting
Actor Razzie for his performance as Lex Luthor,

00:02:34.799 --> 00:02:39.609
a role that was probably better suited for
someone older and more menacing.

00:02:39.609 --> 00:02:44.042
Honestly, Eisenberg is the worst Luthor of
all time, especially when compared to the

00:02:44.042 --> 00:02:46.900
likes of Gene Hackman and Michael Rosenbaum.

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:52.659
Eisenberg was just too loud and manic to play
the Man of Steel's archenemy, and honestly,

00:02:52.659 --> 00:02:55.980
he would've done a far better job as the Riddler
or the Joker.

00:02:55.980 --> 00:03:00.240
Putting a new spin on a classic character
was a risky move on Eisenberg's part, and

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:02.599
sadly, it just didn't pay off.

00:03:02.599 --> 00:03:04.510
“Ding, ding, ding, ding.”

00:03:05.939 --> 00:03:11.189
For more than a decade Halle Berry was one
of the most likable stars in Hollywood.

00:03:11.189 --> 00:03:16.189
She starred in comedies like "Boomerang",
serviceable dramas like "Losing Isaiah," and

00:03:16.189 --> 00:03:18.849
action spectaculars like "X-Men."

00:03:18.849 --> 00:03:25.719
Then, she delivered an absolutely devastating
gut-punch of a performance in the sad, engrossing

00:03:25.719 --> 00:03:28.219
2001 indie movie "Monster's Ball."

00:03:28.219 --> 00:03:33.849
Her work as Leticia Musgrove, the grieving
widow of an executed convict falling in love

00:03:33.849 --> 00:03:38.199
with her husband's executioner, won Berry
an Academy Award for Best Actress.

00:03:38.199 --> 00:03:43.459
But the kind of showy, meaty scripts befitting
a newly-minted Oscar winner didn't follow

00:03:43.459 --> 00:03:44.459
for Berry.

00:03:44.459 --> 00:03:49.290
After "Monster's Ball," Berry was a "Bond
girl" in "Die Another Day," played Storm in

00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:52.849
another X-Men" movie, and co-starred in the
horror flick "Gothika."

00:03:52.849 --> 00:03:58.159
Then, in 2004, she played the titular character
in the Batman spinoff "Catwoman."

00:03:58.159 --> 00:04:03.290
The film consists mainly of Berry strutting
around Gotham City in a tight, flesh-baring

00:04:03.290 --> 00:04:07.229
catsuit, smirking to herself as she commits
crimes and avoids capture.

00:04:07.229 --> 00:04:09.249
Keith Phipps of The AV Club wrote,

00:04:09.249 --> 00:04:14.689
"Catwoman is essentially an excuse to pose
Berry in ever-skimpier outfits."

00:04:14.689 --> 00:04:18.540
Berry at least tried to save this bomb of
a movie and give it her best.

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:20.299
Andrea Thompson of The Spool argued,

00:04:20.299 --> 00:04:24.420
“Some sort of acting category should’ve
been created just for her, and the amount

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:29.130
of effort she spent trying to salvage the
unsalvageable, putting her all into every

00:04:29.130 --> 00:04:30.190
idiotic line.”

00:04:30.190 --> 00:04:32.720
“Oops, my bad.”

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:37.570
Berry herself called "Catwoman" a, quote,
"piece of s---” and a “god-awful movie"

00:04:37.570 --> 00:04:41.240
when she accepted her Razzie Award for Worst
Actress in person.

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:49.020
In the first half of the 1980s, Eddie Murphy
was the hottest star on TV, dominating "Saturday

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:55.270
Night Live" with hilarious characters like
Velvet Jones, Buckwheat, and Mr. Robinson.

00:04:55.270 --> 00:04:59.720
Then he became a major movie star, headlining
classic comedies of the '80s and '90s including

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:04.340
"Beverly Hills Cop," "Trading Places," "Coming
to America," and "The Nutty Professor."

00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:10.150
After some box-office bombs such as "The Adventures
of Pluto Nash" and "I Spy," Eddie Murphy was

00:05:10.150 --> 00:05:13.060
back on top of Hollywood in 2006.

00:05:13.060 --> 00:05:19.960
He earned plaudits for his rare dramatic turn
as a tragic '60s R&amp;B singer in the 2006 movie

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:21.890
adaptation of the musical "Dreamgirls."

00:05:21.890 --> 00:05:26.780
Murphy received his first ever Academy Award
nomination, and he was predicted by Oscars

00:05:26.780 --> 00:05:30.590
pundits and experts to triumphantly take home
a statuette.

00:05:30.590 --> 00:05:35.570
But then "Norbit" came along, apparently derailing
everything.

00:05:35.570 --> 00:05:38.570
“Don’t you think I realize what’s going
on here, miss.”

00:05:38.570 --> 00:05:43.110
Murphy starred in "Norbit" as the titular
character, a nerdy doormat who falls into

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:46.770
an abusive relationship with a mean woman
named Rasputia Latimore.

00:05:46.770 --> 00:05:52.530
With the aid of prosthetics and a cringe-inducing
fake accent, Murphy also portrayed Chinese

00:05:52.530 --> 00:05:58.830
orphanage caretaker Mr. Wong, and, with makeup,
wigs, and a fat suit, played Raputia, too.

00:05:58.830 --> 00:06:05.200
Critics hated the fart joke-heavy "Norbit,"
a film with a 9 percent rating on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:10.220
It's also entirely possible that Academy Award
voters, seeing "Norbit" everywhere, thought

00:06:10.220 --> 00:06:12.460
twice about checking the box for Murphy.

00:06:12.460 --> 00:06:17.970
Alan Arkin ultimately won Best Supporting
Actor for "Little Miss Sunshine" over Murphy.

00:06:19.170 --> 00:06:23.400
In 2003, it had become something of a national
pastime to rag on Gigli.

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:28.990
Part romantic comedy, part mob movie, potentially
insulting to the LGBTQ community, Italians,

00:06:28.990 --> 00:06:34.740
disabled people, and who knows how many other
groups, "Gigli" was indeed a very bad movie.

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:40.180
Jennifer Lopez portrayed Ricki, a charming
gangster sent to help out an inept, low-level

00:06:40.180 --> 00:06:45.520
mob goon. Said goon was tasked with kidnapping
the mentally disabled brother of a federal

00:06:45.520 --> 00:06:47.700
prosecutor going after their syndicate.

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:53.890
Ricki is gay, but she somehow can't resist
the charms of Affleck's Gigli — a sputtering,

00:06:53.890 --> 00:06:56.530
clownish, Italian-American stereotype.

00:06:56.530 --> 00:07:01.560
The Independent Critic found Affleck so terrific
in "Argo," "Gone Girl," "The Way Back," and

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:04.200
"Good Will Hunting," as did many others.

00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:10.330
So to see, as the outlet put it, that Affleck
had no chemistry with Lopez, was disappointing,

00:07:10.330 --> 00:07:13.480
especially since she was his fiancée at the
time.

00:07:13.480 --> 00:07:16.090
Joel Siegel of ABC News boldly wrote,

00:07:16.090 --> 00:07:21.110
"Ben Affleck, an Oscar-winning screenwriter,
is a man of many talents.

00:07:21.110 --> 00:07:22.710
I'm not sure acting is among them."

00:07:23.710 --> 00:07:28.610
In a career filled with silly, high-concept
comedies loathed by critics, Adam Sandler's

00:07:28.610 --> 00:07:33.730
"Jack and Jill" has the impressive distinction
of being the most poorly-reviewed theatrical

00:07:33.730 --> 00:07:35.820
film he has ever made.

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:39.450
It earned a woeful 3 percent on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:07:39.450 --> 00:07:45.550
It was also the first movie to sweep the Razzie
Awards, winning 10 prizes for ineptitude in film.

00:07:45.550 --> 00:07:51.330
Sandler plays the titular twins, both of them
utterly unlikeable, a workaholic commercial

00:07:51.330 --> 00:07:55.650
producer and his obnoxious, loud, emotionally
fragile sister.

00:07:55.650 --> 00:08:01.050
But while Sandler's broad style doesn't feel
out of place in the wacky, physical comedy-oriented

00:08:01.050 --> 00:08:03.610
flick, Al Pacino sure does.

00:08:03.610 --> 00:08:08.290
Pacino works on a completely different level
in the Adam Sandler comedy, surprisingly.

00:08:08.290 --> 00:08:13.500
Known for the intense skills he put to good
use in cinematic touchstones like "Dog Day

00:08:13.500 --> 00:08:18.670
Afternoon," "Serpico" and the "Godfather"
films, those same skills simply paint Pacino

00:08:18.670 --> 00:08:21.340
as unhinged in "Jack and Jill."

00:08:21.340 --> 00:08:27.360
But what's strange is that Pacino's worst
performance has him misplaying ... himself.

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:32.150
The sole motivation of his character to romance
Jill, Adam Sandler in women's clothes and

00:08:32.150 --> 00:08:35.169
a wig, leaves him with nothing to work with.

00:08:35.169 --> 00:08:39.930
All we see is a perverse fascination with
an utterly unappealing woman.

00:08:39.930 --> 00:08:44.879
Pacino aggressively makes unwanted advances
toward Jill, groping her, touching her, and

00:08:44.879 --> 00:08:47.410
trying to show her how to play Brooklyn-style
stickball.

00:08:47.410 --> 00:08:52.950
Though, he does take breaks to go off on long,
meandering, mumbled tangents about his life,

00:08:52.950 --> 00:08:58.320
work, and wealth with a wide and wild-eyed
stare that uncomfortably penetrates other

00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:00.449
characters and the audience, too.

00:09:01.649 --> 00:09:07.110
After starring in masterpieces like "Taxi
Driver," "The Godfather: Part II," "The Deer

00:09:07.110 --> 00:09:12.300
Hunter," and "Raging Bull," Robert De Niro
built up a legendary career as one of the

00:09:12.300 --> 00:09:15.160
most respected actors of his generation.

00:09:15.160 --> 00:09:19.139
He followed that up by spending most of the
new millennium playing grumpy old men.

00:09:19.139 --> 00:09:22.879
This strategy may have worked out commercially
with "Meet the Parents" and its sequels, but

00:09:22.879 --> 00:09:29.279
not so much with "The Big Wedding," "Everybody's
Fine," "The War with Grandpa," or "Dirty Grandpa."

00:09:29.279 --> 00:09:35.029
De Niro, so frequently the orchestrator of
singular roles with singular talent and detail,

00:09:35.029 --> 00:09:40.620
has been reduced to portraying the cliched
lusty old man who wants to party hard and

00:09:40.620 --> 00:09:42.180
make Viagra jokes.

00:09:42.180 --> 00:09:47.589
He tricks his buttoned-up grandson into taking
him to Daytona Beach for spring break festivities.

00:09:47.589 --> 00:09:52.259
He is, in other words, exactly what the title
says he is: a dirty grandpa.

00:09:52.259 --> 00:09:54.860
Flavorwire's Jason Bailey called the movie,

00:09:54.860 --> 00:10:01.480
"The absolute nadir of late-period Robert
De Niro, which is really saying something,"

00:10:01.480 --> 00:10:02.800
Critic Richard Crouse said,

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:07.399
"[It’s] the kind of film that, one day,
De Niro's great-grandchildren will watch and

00:10:07.399 --> 00:10:09.790
wonder what all the fuss about him was."

00:10:10.839 --> 00:10:16.220
Jon Voight‘s career has spanned seven decades,
beginning in the 1960s and exploding with

00:10:16.220 --> 00:10:22.079
an Oscar-nominated breakout role as a naive
sex worker in the 1969 Best Picture winner

00:10:22.079 --> 00:10:23.079
"Midnight Cowboy."

00:10:23.079 --> 00:10:28.279
A decade later, he gave voice to the experience
of hundreds of thousands of American men as

00:10:28.279 --> 00:10:32.730
a Vietnam War veteran readjusting to civilian
life in "Coming Home," for which he won the

00:10:32.730 --> 00:10:34.089
Oscar for Best Actor.

00:10:34.089 --> 00:10:40.670
In 2001, Voight was nominated again for playing
Howard Cosell in the Muhammad Ali biopic "Ali."

00:10:40.670 --> 00:10:45.260
In recent years, Voight has turned to TV,
earning accolades and awards for his roles

00:10:45.260 --> 00:10:47.490
on "24" and "Ray Donovan."

00:10:47.490 --> 00:10:50.360
But Voight doesn't only appear in prestigious
projects.

00:10:50.360 --> 00:10:56.920
He's a prolific actor who has sometimes, unapologetically,
taken paychecks wherever they present themselves.

00:10:56.920 --> 00:11:04.950
Certainly, the rock bottom of his resume of
100 projects is "Superbabies: Baby Geniuses 2."

00:11:04.950 --> 00:11:09.980
Voight plays the villainous Bill Biscane,
a German tycoon with a bad blond wig, locked

00:11:09.980 --> 00:11:13.230
in an endless battle with a James Bond-type
baby.

00:11:13.230 --> 00:11:18.060
"Superbabies" scored a perfect 0 percent on
Rotten Tomatoes, with many critics blaming

00:11:18.060 --> 00:11:21.040
the film's awfulness squarely on Voight.

00:11:21.040 --> 00:11:26.399
The Austin Chronicle called his performance
that of a superhammy nincompoop, while the

00:11:26.399 --> 00:11:30.529
San Francisco Chronicle's critic said they
felt embarrassed for the actor.

00:11:30.529 --> 00:11:35.600
USA Today said that Voight looked more comfortable
when he was regurgitated in the snake-based

00:11:35.600 --> 00:11:37.029
horror movie "Anaconda."

00:11:38.949 --> 00:11:45.120
"Theodore Rex" is a movie so strange, misbegotten,
and largely forgotten that it feels like a

00:11:45.120 --> 00:11:49.439
fake movie you'd see in a real one making
fun of the entertainment industry.

00:11:49.439 --> 00:11:55.550
But no, Whoopi Goldberg really did star in
a gritty police movie about a human and a

00:11:55.550 --> 00:12:00.939
dinosaur, paired up as detectives trying to
solve a crime in a world where people and

00:12:00.939 --> 00:12:03.850
talking dinosaurs co-exist.

00:12:03.850 --> 00:12:08.380
Whoopi Goldberg played the human half of the
detective duo in "Theodore Rex."

00:12:08.380 --> 00:12:15.339
Her performance as a futuristic police officer,
acting opposite a pretend dinosaur, does not

00:12:15.339 --> 00:12:21.040
rank with her iconic roles, such as in "The
Color Purple," "Ghost," or "Sister Act."

00:12:21.040 --> 00:12:27.980
Goldberg seems alternately bored, annoyed,
anxious, and wooden, and with good reason.

00:12:27.980 --> 00:12:33.470
After trying to back out of a promise to star
in the movie, producers sued Goldberg in 1993.

00:12:33.470 --> 00:12:38.967
After much legal wrangling, the actor was
essentially forced by the courts to act in "Theodore Rex."

00:12:38.967 --> 00:12:45.179
Looking at her performance all these years
later, it sure seems like she was determined

00:12:45.179 --> 00:12:48.699
to put in as little effort as humanly possible.

00:12:48.699 --> 00:12:49.819
“Thank you, sir.”

00:12:50.819 --> 00:12:56.360
"Cats" is an extremely theatrical experience,
which is probably why it took so long to make

00:12:56.360 --> 00:12:57.459
it into a movie.

00:12:57.459 --> 00:13:03.510
The 2019 film's sprawling cast included nearly
80 actors, many of whom have won all sorts

00:13:03.510 --> 00:13:04.679
of awards.

00:13:04.679 --> 00:13:09.639
And what did director Tom Hooper do with so
many talented actors, including Dame Judi

00:13:09.639 --> 00:13:11.329
Dench, Jennifer Hudson, and Idris Elba?

00:13:11.329 --> 00:13:16.680
He dressed them up in shabby cat costumes
and had them prance around a soundstage while

00:13:16.680 --> 00:13:21.730
singing nonsensical songs about cat life,
while CGI technology shrank them down to cat size.

00:13:21.730 --> 00:13:25.060
The whole idea should have been relegated
to the litter box.

00:13:25.060 --> 00:13:30.370
"Cats" features such hard-to-watch scenes
as McKellen, best known as the mighty Gandalf

00:13:30.370 --> 00:13:36.338
in the iconic "The Lord of the Rings" films,
lamenting his glory days as a young theater cat.

00:13:36.338 --> 00:13:43.129
"Cats" was nominated for nine Razzie Awards,
with Dench singled out for her particularly

00:13:43.129 --> 00:13:45.550
imperfect performance.

